A||||4||BENGAL4||CLUTCH MASTER CYL LEAK?
Z||000000||BENGAL4||07-26-99||02:35 PM||||Latetly, I have had to add fluid to the clutch master cylinder. I looked for leaks on the garage floor but saw none. I looked under the hood and still no fluid. Puzzled, I then looked on the carpeting by the pedals and there was the leak. All over the carpet. My question is should I get a new master cylinder or try and rebuild the one I have. I am installing new seat covers and carpet so I want to make sure that this is the problem. Any suggestions?<p><br>Bill
Z||000001||PAWPAW||07-26-99||04:53 PM||||I tried to do the rebuild way.  &lt;ost of the time it will not work properly and will leak.  Go with a parts store rebuilt one.  Perfrom the gravity type air bleed.  I tried the foot on pedal.pressure  method and vacuum pumps.  This method was recommended by a Saturn Mechanic.
Z||000002||Patrick||07-26-99||05:36 PM||||<br>PAWPAW, possibly you missed it the first time I asked, but what is the "gravity method" you've mentioned before?<p>
Z||000003||JetroGT||07-27-99||01:15 AM||||When you install your new carpeting you will probably have to reprime and repaint the metal surface where the clutch fluid leaked onto. It makes for a good paint stripper.
Z||000004||ChadMan||07-27-99||07:23 AM||||I'd go with a parts-store master cylinder.  The last time I had to replace one was in 1996.  I got a NAPA unit for $90 for my 88 coupe and it's performed flawlessly for 31,000 miles now (knock on wood).<p>You'd probably do just as good a job in your garage, but for $90, half an hour for the installation and an hour to bleed it, you'll be rolling again.
